            <ArticleTitle>Activity-related knee injuries and pain in athletic adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>146-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>By collecting data from 45 students at a ski high school, we found that a total of 73% of the students reported activity-related pain/injuries of the knee. Sixty-one percent had overuse injuries, 27% malalignment, and 12% had indistinct knee pain. Females suffered more knee pain/injuries (88%) than males (57%). Significantly higher Q-angle degrees were recorded for females (16) than for males (10). &quot;Jumper's knee&quot; was found in all competitive students with a KT manual maximum difference (MMD) of 3 mm or more (mean 4 mm), with a hard endpoint, whereas this was less common among the other competitive students (P &lt; 0.05). The students were given counselling about training and physiotherapy. In the follow-up study 1 year later, a significant reduction of knee pain/overuse injuries, from 73% to 35%, was recorded. This may be related to better equipment, the development of techniques, and training of the muscles. A high volume of training and knee instability, with MMD of 3 mm or more, seemed to be correlated with an increased risk for &quot;jumper's knee&quot; and, possibly, for skiing injuries. By identifying those at increased risk, preseason recommendations can be made and ski injuries may be prevented.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
